= Complex Analysis and Spectral Theory

== Cauchy Residue Theorem and Contour Integration

Let $f: Omega -> CC$ be meromorphic on a simply connected domain $Omega$ with isolated poles $\{z_1, dots, z_k\}$ inside a positively oriented simple closed contour $gamma$. The Cauchy Residue Theorem states:

$ integral.cont_gamma f(z) d z = 2 pi i sum_(j=1)^k "Res"(f, z_j) $

For a pole of order $m$ at $z_0$, the residue is computed analytically via:

$ "Res"(f, z_0) = 1 / ((m - 1)!) lim_(z -> z_0) (d^(m - 1))/(d z^(m - 1)) [ (z - z_0)^m f(z) ] $

== Sokhotski-Plemelj Theorem and Green's Resolvent

In scattering theory, evaluating matrix elements along the real energy axis involves the singular distribution limit:

$ lim_(epsilon -> 0^+) 1 / (x - x_0 plus.minus i epsilon) = cal(P) (1 / (x - x_0)) minus.plus i pi delta(x - x_0) $

where $cal(P)$ denotes Cauchy principal value integration.

== Spectral Decomposition of Self-Adjoint Operators

Let $A$ be a densely defined self-adjoint operator on a separable Hilbert space $cal(H)$. According to the spectral theorem, there exists a unique projection-valued measure $E_A$ such that:

$ A = integral_(-infinity)^infinity lambda d E_A (lambda) $

The resolvent operator $R_z(A) = (A - z I)^(-1)$ is holomorphic on the resolvent set $CC backslash sigma(A)$ and satisfies the first resolvent identity:

$ R_(z_1)(A) - R_(z_2)(A) = (z_1 - z_2) R_(z_1)(A) R_(z_2)(A) $

The Stone formula reconstructs spectral projections directly from contour integrals of the resolvent:

$ 1 / 2 [ E_A ([a, b]) + E_A ((a, b)) ] = s-lim_(delta -> 0^+) 1 / (2 pi i) integral_a^b [ R_(lambda - i delta)(A) - R_(lambda + i delta)(A) ] d lambda $

This concludes our analytical survey of structural mathematical physics.
